To solve this requirement while adhering to Salesforce best practices, we must address a critical platform limit: **the maximum number of asynchronous jobs that can be enqueued in a single transaction is 50**. 

If a nightly cleanup closes up to 200 cases at a time through the API, looping through the cases and calling `System.enqueueJob()` for each closed case in the trigger would instantly throw a `LimitException: Too many queueable jobs added to the queue: 51`. 

### Architectural Choice & Solution
To safely handle bulk updates of up to 200 cases (and ensure one failure doesn't affect the others while remaining within governor limits), we implement the following:
1. **Trigger Bulk Safety & Governor Limits**: In `CaseTrigger`, we filter the eligible cases (changed from open to closed, and containing a contact). To ensure we never exceed the 50-queueable-per-transaction limit, we enqueue up to 50 jobs directly in the initial transaction. If there are more than 50 cases, the remaining cases are passed to a chaining mechanism or handled gracefully. Since the requirement specifically asks to "enqueue one job per case" and states that "a nightly cleanup closes up to 200 at a time", we must handle the overflow. 
2. **Queueable Chaining**: We design `CaseSurveyJob` to accept either a single case ID or a list of case IDs. If passed a list, it processes the first case (creating its task), and if there are remaining cases in the list, it enqueues a new instance of itself with the remaining list. This pattern allows us to process 200+ cases individually in their own asynchronous contexts without ever hitting the transaction limit of 50 enqueues.

Here is the complete implementation of the trigger and the Queueable class:

File: force-app/main/default/triggers/CaseTrigger.trigger
```apex
trigger CaseTrigger on Case (after update) {
    List<Id> casesToProcess = new List<Id>();

    for (Case newCase : Trigger.new) {
        Case oldCase = Trigger.oldMap.get(newCase.Id);

        // Check if the case changed from open to closed and has a ContactId
        if (newCase.IsClosed && !oldCase.IsClosed && newCase.ContactId != null) {
            casesToProcess.add(newCase.Id);
        }
    }

    if (!casesToProcess.isEmpty()) {
        // To respect the Governor Limit of 50 enqueued jobs per transaction,
        // we check if we have more than 50 cases. If we do, we delegate the execution
        // to CaseSurveyJob's chaining mechanism to safely process all of them individually.
        if (casesToProcess.size() <= 50) {
            for (Id caseId : casesToProcess) {
                System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(caseId));
            }
        } else {
            // Process the list sequentially via Queueable chaining to avoid LimitException
            System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(casesToProcess));
        }
    }
}
```

