An elegant solution to prevent duplicate Task creation during recursive updates of the same transaction is to track the IDs of the Opportunities for which a task has already been created. Since static variables persist across trigger recursions within the same transaction, a static `Set<Id>` is the perfect mechanism to ensure that at most one `Amount changed` task is produced per Opportunity.

Here is the complete implementation of the trigger action class and its corresponding custom metadata record.

File: force-app/main/default/classes/TA_Opportunity_LogAmountChange.cls
```apex
public with sharing class TA_Opportunity_LogAmountChange implements TriggerAction.AfterUpdate {
    // Static set to keep track of opportunities that have already had an audit task logged in this transaction
    private static Set<Id> processedOppIds = new Set<Id>();

    public void afterUpdate(List<Opportunity> triggerNew, List<Opportunity> triggerOld) {
        Map<Id, Opportunity> oldMap = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(triggerOld);
        List<Task> tasksToInsert = new List<Task>();

        for (Opportunity newOpp : triggerNew) {
            Opportunity oldOpp = oldMap.get(newOpp.Id);

            // Check if this opportunity was already processed during this transaction
            if (processedOppIds.contains(newOpp.Id)) {
                continue;
            }

            // Check if the Amount changed
            if (newOpp.Amount != oldOpp.Amount) {
                tasksToInsert.add(new Task(
                    Subject = 'Amount changed',
                    WhatId = newOpp.Id,
                    Status = 'Completed'
                ));
                // Mark as processed so subsequent recursive trigger loops do not log another task
                processedOppIds.add(newOpp.Id);
            }
        }

        if (!tasksToInsert.isEmpty()) {
            insert tasksToInsert;
        }
    }
}
